00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:33.780
But really, shake--
shake the hand--

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:34.610
hey.

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:41.883
All right--

00:01:41.883 --> 00:01:47.200
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.950
All right, I hope you
met another person.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:54.780
And after this talk, you
can ask them what they do.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:02:00.190
And so what I'll do is I'll
stop with the corny jokes,

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:04.912
and I'll try to answer a couple
of questions, which is really

00:02:04.912 --> 00:02:07.120
one question-- why do we
care about Stream Analytics?

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.250
And why do we need
fresh data, which

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:10.930
is really what this is about?

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:16.300
And then I'll hand this off
to a person who actually

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.240
has had quite a bit of success
with building Stream Analytics

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:20.470
systems on GCP.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:22.930
That's Simon Forman, who is
the head of behavioral data

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:24.280
engineering at ITV.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.500
That's one of England's
premier TV networks.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.110
So he'll tell you a
lot more about that.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:32.620
So let's get into it.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:37.240
Stream Analytics is about
moving from dealing with files--

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.610
to give you a figure,
10 megabytes plus--

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.070
to dealing with one
kilobyte at a time.

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:45.390
As soon as a thing
happens, you process it.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.950
And that requires a bit
of a shift in thinking,

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:50.290
a new kind of technology.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:52.060
You need new storage technology.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.070
You need new
processing technology.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.740
And you need a new way
to access your data.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.610
So on the storage, again,
we've had tremendous amount

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:04.480
of success with technologies for
handling blobs-- large files.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.900
Now you need things like
Cloud Pub/Sub and Apache Kafka

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:11.650
that are great at accepting
appends and persisting them

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.820
on disk at one
kilobyte at a time.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:15.820
There are no more files.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.153
They're kind of infinite, right?

00:03:17.153 --> 00:03:19.210
So you need to be able
to deal with that.

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:22.060
And you need to be able to let
multiple distributed clients

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.730
tail these files without
introducing latency.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:29.020
So that's a new kind
of storage technology.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:32.380
On the processing
side, you have now jobs

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:35.830
that don't just come up,
process a file, and shut down.

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:39.460
You have jobs that run for not
hours, but days, and months,

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.200
and sometimes years.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.170
So you need something that's
stable enough to do this.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:46.690
You need something that can
recover and heal itself when

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:48.810
it's not having a good time.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.740
And you need something
that is going

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:54.968
to be able to help you deal
with data that is unbounded.

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:55.760
There are no files.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.060
There's no end to file.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:00.260
So how do you know when
you've gotten everything?

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.135
It turns out that it's
not a trivial problem.

00:04:02.135 --> 00:04:06.280
So tools like Dataflow
Apache Spark and Flink

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.120
start solving that.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.060
And finally, once you've
done all of this--

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.890
you've done the work
of persisting the data

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:15.070
and processing it in let's say
100 milliseconds or a second.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:16.990
You need a storage
and access system

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:20.740
or a database that is not
going to take all that away.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:23.170
And we talk about
BigQuery streaming API

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:28.180
here, which allows you to
get to your data instantly.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:31.450
So now, why do you
care to make this shift

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:33.520
in your organization?

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.895
There's been a lot of success
just-- many operations team

00:04:35.895 --> 00:04:37.353
that, in fact, will
tell you, well,

00:04:37.353 --> 00:04:39.050
we're very happy just
to have our data.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:40.630
It doesn't matter
that it's fresh.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:42.213
It doesn't matter
that it's streaming.

00:04:42.213 --> 00:04:45.430
Like, if you give me my
data within an hour of it

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:47.140
being generated, I'm great.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:49.060
Why do you care?

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:52.150
There are many
reasons to do this.

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:55.840
And I'll try to focus
on a couple that often

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:01.300
get overlooked, partly because
they are a little bit too

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:02.800
close--

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.500
they seem obvious-- and
partly because, in some ways,

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:09.110
measuring quality service and
sort of saying ops is a bit

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:09.610
too--

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.660
measuring quality of
service has historically

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:17.340
been rather difficult. So
we've kind of glossed over it.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.180
So again, the two reasons to
care about Stream Analytics

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:23.540
is that you need fresh data
on your quality of service.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.340
When things go
wrong with whatever

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.170
it is that your
company does, you

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:29.753
should know about it not in an
hour-- you should know about it

00:05:29.753 --> 00:05:33.590
now, in 10 seconds, in a
minute, but not an hour.

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:36.050
And when you do
know about this, you

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:39.320
need your operations teams,
your SRE team, your content

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:41.510
operations, your
finance operations

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:43.430
to have all of the
supporting data

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.800
to resolve the problem
right there and then.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:47.930
So you need fresh data
on quality of service

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:49.580
and fresh data on operations.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:52.610
So yes, there is some feature
deep in your backlog that's

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:54.650
about flying cars,
and recommendations,

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:58.280
and instant optimization
of everything,

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:00.200
and A/B testing that
is all wonderful.

00:06:02.805 --> 00:06:04.430
But Stream Analytics
is about something

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:05.513
much more basic than that.

00:06:05.513 --> 00:06:06.740
It's quality of service.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:09.217
So I'll try to not--

00:06:09.217 --> 00:06:11.300
I'll try to give you a
more concrete example based

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:14.610
on something that I know and
sort of experienced firsthand,

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.230
which is how we think
about quality of service

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:19.340
at Cloud Pub/Sub in GCP.

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:22.760
And that's generally
representative of how

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:26.050
other services
are run at Google.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:30.470
So when we launched
a few years back,

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.753
we started with a single SLA--

00:06:32.753 --> 00:06:34.670
service level agreement--
that was our promise

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:38.660
to our customers, which was
that when your front ends were

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:42.220
ready to hand us off a piece
of data, an event, a log entry,

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:45.130
we would accept it
with high likelihood.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:47.960
We had 4 1/2 nines of
availability, I think,

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:49.310
when we started.

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:51.020
And so that was our SLA.

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:54.060
And behind it, there was an
SLO, a service level objective,

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:56.750
which is a metric that
was measured in real time.

00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:58.880
And someone's pager
was connected to it.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:01.790
So if all of a sudden you
couldn't publish your data--

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:04.310
your front end set had
accumulated in their memory

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:06.320
and were on their
way to crashing,

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:09.260
someone's pager was going
off and we would fix it.

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:13.040
And then we had a bunch
of low volume probers

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:14.750
that would exercise the system--

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:16.850
sort of different paths
through the system

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:19.620
using synthetic traffic.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.480
Moving on from
there, we've designed

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.190
a brilliant alerting
system which

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:26.270
was to use our users
as a way to get us data

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:28.240
when things were going wrong.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:31.168
So something would go wrong
with your application.

00:07:31.168 --> 00:07:33.460
Let's say you couldn't create
a topic or get your data.

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:36.040
You would submit a ticket
to our support system.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:38.760
It would get triaged by
front line support personnel,

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:40.680
get escalated to
a specialist, get

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.380
escalated to the
engineering team,

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:48.660
and then finally, just short
hours, weeks, days, or weeks

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.552
later, the issue would be fixed.

00:07:51.552 --> 00:07:52.760
This is, of course, sort of--

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:53.802
I'm being facetious here.

00:07:53.802 --> 00:07:55.290
This was a horrible system.

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:58.020
Even that was a step
up for many users.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:02.790
Because oftentimes these systems
within individual organizations

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:05.640
are run by much smaller teams.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:09.450
But certainly, using our
users as an alerting system

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:11.910
or taking hours to
weeks to resolve

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:17.700
issues that went
beyond our primary SLA

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.130
was really no way to live.

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:22.170
And so we've changed
things a bit.

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.750
And we said, OK,
let's understand

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:28.260
our quality of service, actually
the way our users actually

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:29.220
perceive it.

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:30.760
And at the end of
this exercise--

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:33.360
rather sort of at the
stage where we are now,

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:37.740
we went from one primary
user facing SLA or an SLO

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:41.370
backing it to 234
SLOs that are all

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:43.423
connected to someone's pager.

00:08:43.423 --> 00:08:45.090
And so we said, our
promise to the world

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:47.910
is really not that you
can publish a message.

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:50.370
It's not uptime.

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:52.920
It's our entire API.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:55.700
We publish our API
as a [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:56.490
You can see it.

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:58.177
And all of it is a promise.

00:08:58.177 --> 00:09:00.510
So we want to make sure that
the whole thing is working.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:01.885
So we had to look
at availability

00:09:01.885 --> 00:09:04.700
across all operations
in real time.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:07.680
We had to look at not just
the success rate but also

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.520
the latency of operations.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:11.970
We wanted to make sure we
understood when things were not

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:18.450
going right in that respect.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:21.600
Looking deeper at this,
we had to realize that it

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.550
wasn't just about the API.

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:26.670
Our promise to the world
went beyond the API.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.680
It wasn't that you could
publish your message

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:29.882
and receive a message.

00:09:29.882 --> 00:09:32.340
It was that the message would
be delivered from a publisher

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:33.630
to every single subscriber.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:36.833
It turns out that's
not an API method.

00:09:36.833 --> 00:09:38.750
That's something synthetic
you have to compute

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:41.222
by joining multiple API calls.

00:09:41.222 --> 00:09:42.680
So we started
looking at end to end

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:44.930
latency of message delivery.

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:47.360
And then finally, we had
to make all of this data

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:53.120
available so that we could
respond to issues with any

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:57.620
of these SLOs within minutes
and hopefully get to them

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:02.040
before any of our customers
would have to submit a ticket.

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:07.730
So this is what it
looks like today.

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:10.250
I've put some x's there.

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:13.250
You can ask me what
the actual numbers are.

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:15.920
Just as an example, Publish--

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:18.290
I'm giving you an example
of a Publish operations,

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:22.110
but we have the same
thing for others.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:25.850
So we look at the overall
method availability.

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:27.890
We have to normalize
it somehow so

00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:30.800
that successful traffic
from really large customers

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:33.080
doesn't obscure terrible
problems from small volume

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:34.190
customers.

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:36.620
We have to look at it in
each individual region.

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:37.820
So here, there's US West.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:46.430
But we have to have it in every
single region where GCP runs.

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:48.800
And finally, we have to look
at these latency numbers.

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:51.740
And then we repeat this
for every other operation.

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:54.600
I've talked about end
to end latency, right?

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:58.160
We had to measure that-- also
not a straightforward thing

00:10:58.160 --> 00:10:59.610
to do.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:03.180
We had to look at latency
within each individual region.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:05.570
We also make a
rather unique promise

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:08.435
to the world, which is
that we will deliver

00:11:08.435 --> 00:11:12.087
your message to wherever your
clients are from wherever

00:11:12.087 --> 00:11:12.920
they were published.

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:14.240
So we're a global service.

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:16.190
So we had to have
operations-- we have to have

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:18.800
metrics per continent pair.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:21.380
And then finally, we
had to have a few SLOs

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:23.080
for administrative operations.

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:25.095
They're interesting
only in that there

00:11:25.095 --> 00:11:26.720
are different
expectations around them.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:28.130
So SLOs are different.

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:30.860
So having gone
through this exercise,

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:32.820
we realized that--
you know, these

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:34.800
are metrics from this year.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:36.770
And I'm very happy with those.

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:39.500
They weren't like
this when we started.

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:42.980
The color was red
when we started.

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:44.330
And that was the primary--

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:47.060
sort of the primary
impact of this exercise

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:51.510
was to realize that things
are not well and fixing it.

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:54.510
So this is a true humble
brag in that this is a brag.

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:56.510
I'm very excited that we
measure so many things,

00:11:56.510 --> 00:11:58.130
and we're great at them.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:00.440
But this is also a pretty
humbling experience.

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:02.600
Because until we did
this, it turned out

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:05.600
that there were 233 dimensions
of our customer experience

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:09.720
that we had no idea about until
somebody submitted a ticket.

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:12.510
So I bet that's happening
in your organization.

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:14.810
And I think that
that, in my mind,

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:18.560
gets at the core of why Stream
Analytics and fresh data

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:19.650
is important.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:21.590
It's just quality of service.

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:24.440
I'll try to answer
one more question

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:31.010
before I hand this off to Simon
to talk about how you actually

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:34.220
do these things which is--

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:35.420
fine, metrics are great.

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:36.110
SLOs are great.

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:37.700
Everybody read the SRE book--

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:39.320
yes?

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:41.270
Yes, I'm seeing nodding heads.

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:42.740
Great, metrics are great.

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:44.600
But why do you need
to build new things?

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:47.750
Don't we have monitoring
tools like Stackdriver

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:50.270
for operations metrics and
Google Analytics for content

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:51.150
metrics?

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:51.970
Those work.

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:54.680
The answer is, of
course, they do.

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:56.940
And you should use them
for everything you can.

00:12:56.940 --> 00:12:59.780
But I bet you there is
something about your company

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:00.703
that is unique.

00:13:00.703 --> 00:13:02.120
You are in business
because you do

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:06.540
something that is different from
what everybody else is doing.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:09.450
And so there's going to
be that one metric that

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:13.890
is absolutely core to what
your business is that you're

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:15.540
going to need to
compute by yourself

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:17.260
and figure out how to measure.

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:21.630
So in the example of Pub/Sub,
it seemed like we kind of

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:23.130
had it all under control.

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:24.160
There was just an API.

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:26.058
And you know what an
API success rate is.

00:13:26.058 --> 00:13:28.350
But then you get until these
things are like, OK, well,

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:30.470
the API is running
in different regions.

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:32.550
And there are
customer sizes that

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:34.720
vary by multiple
orders of magnitude.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:36.870
How do you normalize them?

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:39.060
How do you make sure that
every customer is happy?

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:40.560
That's not a standard
sort of thing.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.160
That's a value judgment you
have to make for your business.

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:45.660
And then you get into things
like end to end latency, right?

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:48.030
Again, the reason I took
you through that whole story

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:52.320
is to just give you a sense of
the depth you can start getting

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:56.130
at when you start pushing at
the definition of your promise

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:57.360
to the world.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:59.790
And so with end to
end latency, the thing

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:01.970
that was kind of not
obvious at first,

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:04.410
we started and said, all
right, end to end latency

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:06.923
is about a difference
between two timestamps.

00:14:06.923 --> 00:14:08.340
Timestamp a message
when it comes.

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:10.425
And then when somebody
says give me a message,

00:14:10.425 --> 00:14:11.550
you compute the difference.

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:12.870
And it made a real value.

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:16.080
Amazing-- not very
interesting, though.

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:18.390
It turns out that that's
not the whole story.

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:23.070
Because what we promise
is that if you give us

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:25.860
enough opportunities to deliver
a message, we will do that.

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:28.080
But if you're not,
we can't be-- we

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:30.150
can't have our pagers ringing.

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:31.950
That's not our promise.

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:35.172
And so as it turns
out the reasons

00:14:35.172 --> 00:14:37.005
why we may not sometimes
be able to send you

00:14:37.005 --> 00:14:39.090
a message has to do with
your client's state.

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:42.450
So sometimes clients
are under-provisioned.

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:44.820
Maybe you have
messages that require

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:47.370
two machines to process, but
you only have one machine.

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:49.440
Or you have a spike
or something, right?

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:51.900
You don't give us enough
opportunities to deliver.

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:52.740
Messages pile up.

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:54.000
Latency goes up.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:55.300
It's not a systems problem.

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:56.820
It's not us breaking a promise.

00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:58.380
Sometimes there are
bugs in clients.

00:15:01.515 --> 00:15:05.400
You grab-- as a client,
you get a message.

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:06.630
You process it.

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:08.520
And then you crash
before acknowledging,

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:10.950
or for Kafka people, before
committing the offset.

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:15.330
We deliver the message to you.

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:16.830
We're doing everything
right, right?

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:18.690
But the timestamp grows.

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:21.540
Sometimes users choose
not to consume messages

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:23.020
with low latency.

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:25.620
And so all of a sudden, what
this means is that in order

00:15:25.620 --> 00:15:27.570
to compute that
difference of timestamps

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:30.990
you need to join it with some
kind of stream of understanding

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:33.570
of what your clients are doing
for each individual client

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:36.640
and say, well, is this client
in a healthy state or not?

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:40.410
And if it is, then you can
say, OK, we trust this metric.

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:43.950
So this simple
timestamp difference

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:45.210
becomes a complicated thing.

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:48.530
And this is a thing that is
absolutely at the core of what

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:50.260
we do as a service.

00:15:50.260 --> 00:15:53.970
So I hope that gives you a sense
for why Stream Analytics is

00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:56.940
important to something very
basic in your business--

00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:59.510
again, not the thing deep in
the backlog, although that

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:01.080
will come, I'm sure.

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:06.150
And I hope it gives you some
ideas for what you might

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:08.950
compute if you had the tools.

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:12.150
Now I will stop at this.

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:14.490
And I will ask Simon
Forman up on stage

00:16:14.490 --> 00:16:18.275
to tell you about his
journey at ITV in doing this.

00:16:18.275 --> 00:16:21.005
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:24.680
SIMON FORMAN: Thanks Kir.

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:25.500
Hi everyone.

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:28.850
I'm Simon Forman, head of
behavioral data engineering

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:30.655
a ITV.

00:16:30.655 --> 00:16:31.530
So let's start there.

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:33.950
What's behavioral
data engineering?

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:35.750
Well, it's building
and running the systems

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:38.810
that capture, process,
and leverage data

00:16:38.810 --> 00:16:43.040
from our users as they visit
our websites, use our apps,

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:44.990
and watch our shows.

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:48.230
You might think that sounds
like traditional web analytics.

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:50.630
But we actually do a lot
more than that with the data

00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:51.770
that we have.

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:55.010
And I'll talk about that later.

00:16:55.010 --> 00:16:59.800
So to rewind-- three years ago,
we weren't using GCP at all.

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:02.510
And our analytics
data was in the hands

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:07.160
of a supplier with all the
problems that that can bring.

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:10.910
Then I wrote our first
prototype streaming Dataflow job

00:17:10.910 --> 00:17:14.390
and proved that it would work
for our analytics use case.

00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:16.490
But then we had to take
a big leap of faith.

00:17:16.490 --> 00:17:18.890
Although I'd proved
the system would work,

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:21.079
I couldn't predict how
much it would cost to run

00:17:21.079 --> 00:17:23.450
or whether it would scale.

00:17:23.450 --> 00:17:25.099
So we took the risk.

00:17:25.099 --> 00:17:28.099
And I pushed the change to move
all our analytics data over

00:17:28.099 --> 00:17:30.972
to the new system on GCP.

00:17:30.972 --> 00:17:32.430
And now I lead the
engineering team

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:37.220
as we do more and more exciting
things with behavioral data.

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:39.100
ITV's the largest
commercial broadcaster

00:17:39.100 --> 00:17:41.980
in the UK with
broadcast TV channels

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:45.180
and an online streaming service
used by millions of people

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:48.420
everyday.

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:52.050
Last summer, ITV had
two massive events--

00:17:52.050 --> 00:17:54.090
the football World
Cup from Russia

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:58.130
and the return of our
reality TV hit, Love Island.

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:00.140
In the football,
England exceeded

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:02.090
everyone's expectations--

00:18:02.090 --> 00:18:03.920
and our audience
size predictions--

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:07.090
reaching the semi-final stage.

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:10.720
That match was watched by 27
million people on our broadcast

00:18:10.720 --> 00:18:11.600
channel.

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:14.050
That's the biggest
UK TV audience

00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:16.840
since the Olympics in 2012.

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:19.390
And even more impressively,
almost a million people

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:22.840
streamed the match live online,
beating our previous streaming

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:25.950
record many times over.

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:29.000
Love Island also attracted
big audiences every day

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:32.780
of its eight week run, with more
of the young audience choosing

00:18:32.780 --> 00:18:36.380
to stream rather than watch
the broadcast channel.

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:38.040
So both events had
lots of viewers.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:41.280
But the load profiles
were quite different.

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:43.350
Football fans tend
to start watching

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:46.800
during the pregame show, or
even a bit after kickoff,

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:49.230
giving a long ramp up
to the highest audience,

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:52.410
as shown by the blue
line on the graph.

00:18:52.410 --> 00:18:54.060
Love Island's a different story.

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:56.430
The show's in a
broadcast slot with drama

00:18:56.430 --> 00:19:00.180
from the very first minute,
so fans arrive en masse

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:02.070
just as it starts.

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:05.490
The steep green line
shows just how quickly

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:08.340
the audience goes from zero
to hundreds of thousands

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:11.190
in just a couple of minutes.

00:19:11.190 --> 00:19:12.870
These audiences
generate huge amounts

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:15.420
of data for my team's
systems and give us

00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:17.940
the challenge of collecting
it all in real time

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:20.460
and scaling our processing
systems to handle it

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:23.108
without slowing down.

00:19:23.108 --> 00:19:25.650
I'm going to talk about how we
build and run the systems that

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:28.558
handle that data
on GCP, starting

00:19:28.558 --> 00:19:29.850
with our goals for the project.

00:19:33.090 --> 00:19:36.320
Now although we run a
video on demand service,

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:38.570
our busiest events are
actually live streaming.

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:41.450
So we had to be ready
for those surges.

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:44.270
That means the collection
system has to scale quickly.

00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:45.820
But the rest of it
needs to keep up.

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:48.350
Or we'll have angry consumers
waiting for the data

00:19:48.350 --> 00:19:50.330
that they need.

00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:53.480
In the near future, we'll
have to handle a lot more data

00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:55.730
as people continue to
shift their attention

00:19:55.730 --> 00:20:00.790
to online streaming and we
incorporate other data sources.

00:20:00.790 --> 00:20:03.470
We want to avoid growing
the team at the same rate.

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:06.820
So we lean heavily on managed
services with low operational

00:20:06.820 --> 00:20:08.710
support requirements.

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:13.010
BigQuery and Cloud Functions are
really good examples of this.

00:20:13.010 --> 00:20:15.030
We want people to
trust our data--

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:18.220
trust that it's complete
and that it's accurate.

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:19.840
And we want the
front end engineering

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:22.120
teams to have an easy
job of implementing

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:25.100
data reporting for us.

00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:27.170
And finally, we want our
downstream consumers,

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:29.690
like data scientists,
to be able to use

00:20:29.690 --> 00:20:33.280
the data we share with them
as a foundation for their work

00:20:33.280 --> 00:20:37.370
without doing any tedious
cleanup beforehand.

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:39.950
GCP, and especially
Pub/Sub and BigQuery,

00:20:39.950 --> 00:20:42.230
make it very easy to deploy
new applications that

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:45.882
use data without any
risk to existing ones.

00:20:45.882 --> 00:20:47.840
Now I'm going to show
you some examples of what

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:51.770
our systems actually look like,
starting with the one that

00:20:51.770 --> 00:20:54.100
receives data from our users.

00:20:54.100 --> 00:20:56.780
All of our apps and
sites have embedded

00:20:56.780 --> 00:21:00.200
code that sends small pieces
of data back to simple receiver

00:21:00.200 --> 00:21:03.150
services using HDTV.

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:05.400
These services are
either Cloud Functions

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:08.620
or run on Compute Engine.

00:21:08.620 --> 00:21:11.010
The one that handles
video playback events has

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:14.080
to be able to cope with
sudden surges from people

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:16.120
watching live events.

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:18.310
So we use sensitively
configured auto scaling

00:21:18.310 --> 00:21:20.410
to handle those highs
without spending

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:23.320
a fortune on excess capacity.

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:24.880
This service does
minimal processing

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:26.980
of the events it receives.

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:29.800
The strategy is to get
them onto a Pub/Sub topic

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:32.620
as quickly as possible.

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:34.590
Having everything
go through Pub/Sub

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:37.950
means we can plug-in different
consumers very easily.

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:42.640
In one case, we have 13
consumers for one topic.

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:44.500
On the right of
this diagram there's

00:21:44.500 --> 00:21:47.460
a Dataflow job
writing to BigQuery.

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:50.750
This stores everything
in its raw format.

00:21:50.750 --> 00:21:52.980
And it's a pattern that
we repeat on most topics,

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:55.990
whether they're dealing with
raw data, like this case,

00:21:55.990 --> 00:21:58.690
or aggregated.

00:21:58.690 --> 00:22:01.490
Often these are archives
needed for audit purposes.

00:22:01.490 --> 00:22:03.340
So we have to store it.

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:06.730
But it's also been very helpful
for debugging or to replay

00:22:06.730 --> 00:22:10.450
historical data
through a new job.

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:14.440
We considered file-based storage
for this instead of BigQuery.

00:22:14.440 --> 00:22:17.050
But there wasn't much
difference in the cost.

00:22:17.050 --> 00:22:20.890
And having the data online in
a way that was easily queried

00:22:20.890 --> 00:22:22.030
was a really nice benefit.

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:29.570
Now moving onto a
more complex system,

00:22:29.570 --> 00:22:32.840
this diagram shows the flow
of video playback and site

00:22:32.840 --> 00:22:36.500
interaction data through
two Dataflow jobs that first

00:22:36.500 --> 00:22:39.400
summarize then sessionize it.

00:22:39.400 --> 00:22:42.230
Summarizing means turning
possibly hundreds of events

00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:45.110
from one video stream
into a single summary

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:46.670
of the important bits--

00:22:46.670 --> 00:22:48.980
who the user was, what
show they watched,

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:52.220
how long they watched
it for, and so on.

00:22:52.220 --> 00:22:54.990
This job's data source
is the same topic

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:58.790
the ingest service on the
previous slide was pushing to.

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:02.290
Sessionizing is pulling together
all of the video summaries

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:04.750
and site interactions
for each user session

00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:08.143
and delivering them
downstream as a bundle.

00:23:08.143 --> 00:23:10.310
This job reads summaries
from the summarizers output

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:13.940
topic and raw site interaction
data from another one

00:23:13.940 --> 00:23:17.510
and publishes to its
own output topic.

00:23:17.510 --> 00:23:19.040
Hanging off the
sessionizers topic

00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:22.400
is a Cloud Function that
applies some formatting

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:24.980
before pushing to another topic
that we use as the handoff

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:27.300
to a third party.

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:29.030
This gives them
lots of flexibility

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:32.690
about how they pull that data,
be it streaming, or batch,

00:23:32.690 --> 00:23:35.210
or even push.

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:37.790
In this diagram, you can
also see two BigQuery logging

00:23:37.790 --> 00:23:40.670
jobs that store all
the video summaries

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:42.375
and the sessionized
data in tables.

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:49.030
And now onto a Dataflow job
that gives us real time quality

00:23:49.030 --> 00:23:51.220
of service data.

00:23:51.220 --> 00:23:54.750
This data shows us how our video
streaming service is performing

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:59.990
and lets ITV's operations team
quickly jump on any problem.

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:04.210
The job reads raw video playback
events and aggregates them,

00:24:04.210 --> 00:24:08.020
then publishes to a topic,
typically with just 30 seconds

00:24:08.020 --> 00:24:12.180
of delay between a user having
a problem like re-buffering

00:24:12.180 --> 00:24:15.310
and that being reflected
in the output data.

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:18.070
From the topic, we write
to BigQuery for long term

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:20.540
storage and analysis.

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:24.520
And we push the Splunk for
live dashboards and alerting.

00:24:28.240 --> 00:24:30.710
And in the last example
of architecture,

00:24:30.710 --> 00:24:34.430
I'll show you how the cross
device resume feature works.

00:24:34.430 --> 00:24:36.980
This is a product feature
where you can pick up

00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:39.110
where you're playing
on a different device,

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:43.010
say going from an
iPhone to a smart TV.

00:24:43.010 --> 00:24:45.500
We push a subset of
the same video event

00:24:45.500 --> 00:24:49.820
data using a node
JS app into Redis

00:24:49.820 --> 00:24:54.470
and expose this to the front end
apps through a Cloud Function.

00:24:54.470 --> 00:24:57.180
Latency is absolutely
critical here.

00:24:57.180 --> 00:25:01.380
So we're willing to trade
some reliability to reduce it.

00:25:01.380 --> 00:25:03.260
We were able to
save a heap of time

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:06.470
and built this feature without
adding any position logging

00:25:06.470 --> 00:25:11.220
code to the front end apps by
reusing the data we already had

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:15.312
and building a service to
expose it in a new way.

00:25:15.312 --> 00:25:17.770
So that was a look at some of
the things that we've built--

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:18.900
now onto how we run them.

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:25.480
Our internal customers
care about data accuracy.

00:25:25.480 --> 00:25:27.240
We replaced an old
analytics system.

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:31.020
And we have to be at least as
good as it was by that measure.

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:33.127
They want data
without any delay so

00:25:33.127 --> 00:25:35.460
that they can use it to power
real time product features

00:25:35.460 --> 00:25:38.320
and drive operational reporting.

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:40.900
And they want us to quickly
build new applications

00:25:40.900 --> 00:25:44.710
and incorporate more data
sources into existing ones.

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:46.830
And to support this,
we have metrics--

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:48.640
SLIs that we watch.

00:25:48.640 --> 00:25:50.960
At the ingest stage, we
don't want any errors.

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:53.260
We want to collect all
the data and get it safely

00:25:53.260 --> 00:25:55.150
onto a Pub/Sub topic.

00:25:55.150 --> 00:25:57.650
And we want that data
to be high quality.

00:25:57.650 --> 00:26:00.220
It needs to match the
spec for required fields

00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:02.920
and suitable values.

00:26:02.920 --> 00:26:06.100
Once we have the data
and it's well-formed,

00:26:06.100 --> 00:26:08.303
we need to process it quickly.

00:26:08.303 --> 00:26:09.970
And a good way to
check that's happening

00:26:09.970 --> 00:26:12.053
is look at the size of the
backlogs on the Pub/Sub

00:26:12.053 --> 00:26:14.570
subscriptions we use.

00:26:14.570 --> 00:26:17.280
And lastly, saturation--
it's important to make

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:19.740
sure we're not getting
close to any capacity limits

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:24.555
to avoid delaying, or even
worse, losing any data.

00:26:24.555 --> 00:26:26.430
Now I'll show you some
of the dashboards that

00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:29.000
demonstrate this.

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:31.340
The first one shows how
we monitor the video data

00:26:31.340 --> 00:26:33.170
summarizer job.

00:26:33.170 --> 00:26:36.080
You can think of this job in
classical input process output

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:37.220
terms.

00:26:37.220 --> 00:26:40.250
It pulls messages from a Pub/Sub
subscription-- the input--

00:26:40.250 --> 00:26:41.630
applies some business logic--

00:26:41.630 --> 00:26:45.450
that's the process-- then
outputs video summaries.

00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:49.400
The graphs here show how each
of those stages is behaving.

00:26:49.400 --> 00:26:51.080
The two on the far
left show the rate

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:53.890
that data is being
published to the input topic

00:26:53.890 --> 00:26:55.730
and how much of
that hasn't yet been

00:26:55.730 --> 00:26:58.650
consumed by the Dataflow job.

00:26:58.650 --> 00:27:00.500
A backlog here would
indicate the job

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:02.840
not keeping up with
the source data rate,

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:06.140
and consequently
delaying its output.

00:27:06.140 --> 00:27:07.640
But the spikes in
the graph here are

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:10.380
short lived, just a
couple of minutes,

00:27:10.380 --> 00:27:12.590
and are caused by the job
auto scaling up and down.

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:15.710
So they're not something
to worry about.

00:27:15.710 --> 00:27:17.420
In the higher middle
graph, there's

00:27:17.420 --> 00:27:19.940
a view of the
Dataflow job's lag,

00:27:19.940 --> 00:27:23.380
which is a measure of the oldest
data still being processed.

00:27:23.380 --> 00:27:28.660
If this is elevated, output
data will again be delayed.

00:27:28.660 --> 00:27:32.980
Below that is what the auto
scaling system is doing.

00:27:32.980 --> 00:27:35.440
We want this to roughly move
in line with the input data

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:36.160
volume.

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:39.230
If it isn't, that could be
an indication of a problem--

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:41.290
for example, hitting
the maximum but still

00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:44.900
needing more
resources to keep up.

00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:47.830
Top right is a custom metric.

00:27:47.830 --> 00:27:49.690
We added code to
the Dataflow job

00:27:49.690 --> 00:27:53.740
to increment a counter
whenever a condition is hit.

00:27:53.740 --> 00:27:56.410
In this case, it's counting
malformed source data--

00:27:56.410 --> 00:27:58.570
data that we can't
process, because it doesn't

00:27:58.570 --> 00:28:00.520
have all the fields we need.

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:03.610
We want this to stay a low
fraction of the input rate.

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:05.800
If it deviates
from that trend, we

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:09.270
could have a problem
with our data sources.

00:28:09.270 --> 00:28:11.170
And lastly, the
lower right graph

00:28:11.170 --> 00:28:13.620
shows how many summaries
are being produced--

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:15.700
the final output stage.

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:17.820
A drop here means the
job isn't producing

00:28:17.820 --> 00:28:21.120
the data it should be.

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:23.940
Taken together, these graphs
show the job's overall health,

00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:26.415
and if there's a problem,
what the likely cause is.

00:28:30.590 --> 00:28:33.080
This dashboard looks at
one of our ingest systems--

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:35.435
the part that handles the
fire hose of data coming in

00:28:35.435 --> 00:28:38.190
from our apps and sites.

00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:41.840
I'm a big fan of the SRE
book's four golden signals--

00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:43.850
traffic, latency,
errors, and saturation.

00:28:43.850 --> 00:28:45.710
And they're monitored here.

00:28:45.710 --> 00:28:47.750
At the top left, there's
a view of the request

00:28:47.750 --> 00:28:52.270
rate, split by response code.

00:28:52.270 --> 00:28:56.020
Any problems with the service
would show up here as 500s.

00:28:56.020 --> 00:29:00.500
There usually aren't any
though, so that hugs the x-axis.

00:29:00.500 --> 00:29:03.250
Below that is the
service's latency--

00:29:03.250 --> 00:29:05.860
how quickly it's publishing
the piece of data to Pub/Sub

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:08.410
and returning an
OK back to the app.

00:29:08.410 --> 00:29:10.300
The apps aren't actually
latency sensitive.

00:29:10.300 --> 00:29:13.300
And we're not affecting the user
experience at all by being slow

00:29:13.300 --> 00:29:14.710
here.

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:17.200
But increased latency
does drastically

00:29:17.200 --> 00:29:18.850
reduce the service's capacity.

00:29:18.850 --> 00:29:20.590
So it could turn
into a full outage

00:29:20.590 --> 00:29:24.440
if it continues or
becomes more widespread.

00:29:24.440 --> 00:29:27.230
The orange, green,
and purple graph

00:29:27.230 --> 00:29:29.720
shows the service's
own view of how quickly

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:34.780
Pub/Sub is accepting data,
again taken from the SRE book.

00:29:34.780 --> 00:29:38.660
The time each Publish operation
takes is bucketed and counted.

00:29:38.660 --> 00:29:40.790
This gives a high
resolution view

00:29:40.790 --> 00:29:42.530
without storing and
graphing millions

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:44.750
of data points every minute.

00:29:44.750 --> 00:29:47.600
Everything in the
orange region and above

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:49.580
is quicker than
100 milliseconds,

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:51.260
with just a small
number of requests

00:29:51.260 --> 00:29:52.580
taking longer than that.

00:29:52.580 --> 00:29:55.961
You might see them in the
lower right area of the graph.

00:29:55.961 --> 00:29:59.980
And lastly, saturation-- the
two graphs in the top right

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:03.100
are from the auto scaling
group's monitoring.

00:30:03.100 --> 00:30:05.960
Obviously, hitting the upper
configured limit is bad.

00:30:05.960 --> 00:30:09.670
But we also want to see
the instance count, CPU,

00:30:09.670 --> 00:30:13.870
and request rate roughly
moving in sync with each other.

00:30:17.300 --> 00:30:19.240
And this last
dashboard shows how

00:30:19.240 --> 00:30:21.950
we monitor the data
feed out to our partner.

00:30:21.950 --> 00:30:24.700
A Pub/Sub subscription
is the boundary

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:26.590
between our two organizations.

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:31.350
We publish data to it,
and our partner pulls it.

00:30:31.350 --> 00:30:33.930
So the key metrics are the
publish rate and the pull rate

00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:35.370
on the top row.

00:30:35.370 --> 00:30:38.310
If these stay balanced,
we won't get a backlog.

00:30:38.310 --> 00:30:40.350
And data won't be delayed.

00:30:40.350 --> 00:30:42.630
If a backlog has
grown, the lower two

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:46.140
graphs show how much data
and how many messages

00:30:46.140 --> 00:30:48.960
is waiting to be consumed.

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:52.260
The spike in the center of the
graphs was less of a problem

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:54.200
than its shape suggests.

00:30:54.200 --> 00:30:56.520
And the backlog was only a
few seconds worth of data.

00:30:56.520 --> 00:31:00.290
And it cleared quite quickly.

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:03.060
So that was how we take
care of running systems.

00:31:03.060 --> 00:31:06.470
But how do we deploy code
when streaming jobs run 24/7?

00:31:09.260 --> 00:31:11.850
Things are a bit
different with streaming.

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:14.480
Updates, and Cloud Functions,
and simple Compute Engine apps

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:16.017
is easy.

00:31:16.017 --> 00:31:17.600
There's no state,
so they're naturally

00:31:17.600 --> 00:31:20.180
easy to change online.

00:31:20.180 --> 00:31:23.930
Dataflow jobs that do hold
state are much harder.

00:31:23.930 --> 00:31:25.910
Stopping and starting
isn't an option,

00:31:25.910 --> 00:31:30.750
as that would lose some output
data or introduce inaccuracy.

00:31:30.750 --> 00:31:33.780
The best solution is to live
update the running job using

00:31:33.780 --> 00:31:35.970
Dataflow's built in feature.

00:31:35.970 --> 00:31:37.452
This is quick and easy.

00:31:37.452 --> 00:31:38.910
In fact, we have
Dataflow jobs that

00:31:38.910 --> 00:31:43.510
are updated automatically by our
continuous deployment system.

00:31:43.510 --> 00:31:46.450
But it's sometimes not possible
to use this feature if there's

00:31:46.450 --> 00:31:49.330
a significant difference
in the job structure

00:31:49.330 --> 00:31:52.130
or the code's not compatible.

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:55.810
So we needed a smarter way to
release fixes and improvements

00:31:55.810 --> 00:31:58.900
without stopping the job.

00:31:58.900 --> 00:32:01.270
Our solution for the
video summarizer process

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:04.090
is to start the updated job
while the old one is still

00:32:04.090 --> 00:32:05.560
running.

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:07.900
The new one gets its
own input subscription,

00:32:07.900 --> 00:32:11.200
so it'll get a copy of the
same data as the old one.

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:13.730
But having two running
causes a problem.

00:32:13.730 --> 00:32:16.530
The new job will also
be emitting data,

00:32:16.530 --> 00:32:19.270
at best causing
duplication, or if it

00:32:19.270 --> 00:32:21.130
doesn't have the
full state, producing

00:32:21.130 --> 00:32:23.700
data that's just incorrect.

00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:26.270
We needed a way for
the new job to wait

00:32:26.270 --> 00:32:30.050
until it had all the
state it needed before it

00:32:30.050 --> 00:32:31.930
started outputting anything.

00:32:31.930 --> 00:32:35.990
And we needed the old job
to stop producing data

00:32:35.990 --> 00:32:44.160
at exactly the same time so
that there was no duplication.

00:32:44.160 --> 00:32:47.250
Synchronizing the two
jobs looked very complex

00:32:47.250 --> 00:32:50.440
and couldn't even guarantee
exactly one's output.

00:32:50.440 --> 00:32:52.410
So we looked for a way
for them to coordinate

00:32:52.410 --> 00:32:56.610
without any direct
communication.

00:32:56.610 --> 00:32:59.280
The thing we came up with
was to use a characteristic

00:32:59.280 --> 00:33:03.570
of the output data here at the
start time of the video stream

00:33:03.570 --> 00:33:05.880
as the deciding factor.

00:33:05.880 --> 00:33:08.610
We pick a point in
time and say any data

00:33:08.610 --> 00:33:11.040
from before then comes
from the old job,

00:33:11.040 --> 00:33:15.340
and anything after that
point comes from the new job.

00:33:15.340 --> 00:33:17.590
To implement this, we
added a feature to the job

00:33:17.590 --> 00:33:20.560
where it could conditionally
drop data instead of publishing

00:33:20.560 --> 00:33:23.080
it to the output topic.

00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:26.950
It still reads all the source
data, applies the same logic--

00:33:26.950 --> 00:33:30.158
it just doesn't
publish the result.

00:33:30.158 --> 00:33:32.200
So let's go through the
whole deployment process.

00:33:32.200 --> 00:33:34.800
To start with, the old
version is running.

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:37.470
That's the green line.

00:33:37.470 --> 00:33:41.790
And it's emitting data to the
output topic, the green boxes.

00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:44.020
Then we deploy the new
version, the blue line,

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:47.670
and a specify a cut-over time
some hours in the future.

00:33:47.670 --> 00:33:52.140
And we reconfigure the old job
with the same cut-over time.

00:33:52.140 --> 00:33:54.900
We'll be able to see metrics
from the new job showing

00:33:54.900 --> 00:33:57.190
that it's working as expected.

00:33:57.190 --> 00:34:01.650
But if it isn't, we can just
stop it and look into why.

00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:05.190
When data is produced for
after the cut-over time,

00:34:05.190 --> 00:34:07.240
it'll come from the new job.

00:34:07.240 --> 00:34:09.929
That's the blue
boxes on the diagram.

00:34:09.929 --> 00:34:12.580
After a while, the old job
won't be producing anything.

00:34:12.580 --> 00:34:16.580
So we can stop it and
clear up its subscription.

00:34:16.580 --> 00:34:20.600
So that's a quick tour
of our current work.

00:34:20.600 --> 00:34:22.929
What about the future?

00:34:22.929 --> 00:34:25.179
So we already support
personalization features

00:34:25.179 --> 00:34:26.449
across our apps.

00:34:26.449 --> 00:34:28.270
And we're going to
build even more of them,

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:31.500
often using the same
data we already have.

00:34:31.500 --> 00:34:34.139
We're working on ad tech to help
our commercial teams deliver

00:34:34.139 --> 00:34:37.630
more value to our advertisers.

00:34:37.630 --> 00:34:40.020
Our platform provides a
way for data scientists

00:34:40.020 --> 00:34:43.110
to quickly and easily put
their research into production

00:34:43.110 --> 00:34:45.090
and work on challenges
like improving marketing

00:34:45.090 --> 00:34:47.770
effectiveness and better
understanding our audiences

00:34:47.770 --> 00:34:52.320
and how do they use our
products to increase engagement.

00:34:52.320 --> 00:34:54.460
And thanks to the
capacity we have,

00:34:54.460 --> 00:34:58.000
we can begin to collect data
from our enormous broadcast

00:34:58.000 --> 00:35:02.160
audiences, gaining extra
insight into our viewers.

00:35:05.070 --> 00:35:07.110
So to reflect on
what we've achieved,

00:35:07.110 --> 00:35:09.400
we provide high quality data.

00:35:09.400 --> 00:35:11.670
Quality is embedded
throughout the pipeline

00:35:11.670 --> 00:35:14.670
from how we build our front
end apps through validating

00:35:14.670 --> 00:35:18.420
the data we receive to checking
that what we're producing

00:35:18.420 --> 00:35:20.210
is correct.

00:35:20.210 --> 00:35:23.210
Our data is trusted by
the people who use it.

00:35:23.210 --> 00:35:24.440
They know it's accurate.

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:27.590
And they happily rely on it.

00:35:27.590 --> 00:35:29.170
It's available immediately.

00:35:29.170 --> 00:35:32.600
Our streaming jobs deliver
useful process data

00:35:32.600 --> 00:35:33.820
without any delay.

00:35:33.820 --> 00:35:38.120
And we support real time
personalization features.

00:35:38.120 --> 00:35:41.440
Last summer, we had
more online viewers

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:43.850
and successfully
collected many times

00:35:43.850 --> 00:35:47.330
more data than ever before
while maintaining our latency

00:35:47.330 --> 00:35:49.680
and reliability SLOs.

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:52.760
And we did that without
any re-engineering.

00:35:52.760 --> 00:35:54.380
This gives us
confidence to handle

00:35:54.380 --> 00:35:58.160
the next tenfold increase as
more people move their viewing

00:35:58.160 --> 00:36:00.965
online and we begin to collect
data from broadcast services.

00:36:03.800 --> 00:36:06.470
What started as an experiment
with one Dataflow job

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:08.900
has turned into a mature
data platform that

00:36:08.900 --> 00:36:14.540
supports analytics, operations,
and front end product features

00:36:14.540 --> 00:36:17.020
and sets us up for a future
of more and more data

00:36:17.020 --> 00:36:19.110
based applications.

00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:19.610
Thank you.

00:36:19.610 --> 00:36:21.260
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:21.260 --> 00:36:22.040
Back to you, Kir.

00:36:28.582 --> 00:36:29.790
KIR TITIEVSKY: Thanks, Simon.

00:36:29.790 --> 00:36:31.230
All right, we're almost there.

00:36:31.230 --> 00:36:34.050
So I hope this gave you
sort of a flavor of what

00:36:34.050 --> 00:36:36.030
that shift to
streaming looks like.

00:36:36.030 --> 00:36:36.840
Yes, it's a shift.

00:36:36.840 --> 00:36:39.960
You have to do a little bit
of work in both how you think

00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:43.280
and how you organize your
systems and engineering.

00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:47.040
But perhaps this makes
it more familiar.

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:52.080
We also hope that this idea
of Stream Analytics being

00:36:52.080 --> 00:36:56.760
core to your quality
of service data

00:36:56.760 --> 00:37:00.450
is an interesting
reason to take back

00:37:00.450 --> 00:37:04.980
to your job and sort of feel
empowered to not just implement

00:37:04.980 --> 00:37:08.520
new features and move data from
databases to databases, which

00:37:08.520 --> 00:37:10.800
is absolutely
critical, but also do

00:37:10.800 --> 00:37:13.500
something that is sort
of fundamental to what

00:37:13.500 --> 00:37:16.333
your company does.

00:37:16.333 --> 00:37:18.000
So clearly, there's
a lot more than that

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:20.880
that you can do
with real time data.

00:37:20.880 --> 00:37:22.590
But that's just one reason.

00:37:22.590 --> 00:37:25.050
Before we part, I wanted to--

00:37:25.050 --> 00:37:27.780
or rather, before
we shift to Q&amp;A,

00:37:27.780 --> 00:37:29.860
I wanted to touch on
a couple of things--

00:37:29.860 --> 00:37:32.670
so a few features
that have to do

00:37:32.670 --> 00:37:37.500
with running Stream Analytics
pipelines in real life

00:37:37.500 --> 00:37:40.350
that have come out in recent
time-- in sort of recent months

00:37:40.350 --> 00:37:43.530
that I wanted to highlight,
as well as a few more sessions

00:37:43.530 --> 00:37:47.160
that will go deeper into some of
the some aspects of this topic

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:50.380
that we haven't gotten
a chance to touch on.

00:37:50.380 --> 00:37:54.420
So the first is the newer
features in Cloud Pub/Sub--

00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:56.550
what's very important for
operating these systems

00:37:56.550 --> 00:38:00.240
is the ability to replay data
to recover from problems.

00:38:00.240 --> 00:38:03.420
If that's not possible,
you are now also

00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:08.310
able to discard messages
or events in bulk.

00:38:08.310 --> 00:38:11.010
Also look out for
upcoming controls

00:38:11.010 --> 00:38:14.130
on where data is stored in
this global service as well

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:18.460
as new ways of
optimizing costs where

00:38:18.460 --> 00:38:23.100
we've split off egress
data and local usage data.

00:38:23.100 --> 00:38:26.340
On Dataflow, stream engine--

00:38:26.340 --> 00:38:28.872
streaming engine, which
has hit GA recently,

00:38:28.872 --> 00:38:30.330
is a very interesting
thing in that

00:38:30.330 --> 00:38:34.710
it offloads a lot of the state
away from individual processing

00:38:34.710 --> 00:38:37.920
nodes, which makes them more
disposable, and so stable,

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:41.800
and also much more flexible
in terms of optimization.

00:38:41.800 --> 00:38:46.320
So streaming auto scaling now
works much more granularly.

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:48.720
And, of course, in real
life, not everybody

00:38:48.720 --> 00:38:52.430
loves Java, which is Dataflow's
primary, or first language.

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:56.800
So Python streaming is
now in beta for Dataflow.

00:38:56.800 --> 00:38:58.500
And if you stick
around the conference,

00:38:58.500 --> 00:39:01.290
there may be a
couple more languages

00:39:01.290 --> 00:39:03.480
that we would be announcing.

00:39:03.480 --> 00:39:06.110
So speaking of that, here's
some sessions you might hit.

